  "resolution_criteria": "This question will resolve as **Yes** if, before July 1, 2027, federal legislation is enacted into law that preempts state regulation of out-of-state pork production.\n\nSpecifically, the enacted text must establish that pork producers may raise and market their products in interstate commerce, free from state or local production-process standards imposed on pigs physically raised outside the state's borders. The statute may also cover other livestock (e.g., cattle, veal, dairy), but pork coverage is what this question turns on.",
  "fine_print": "* Enactment may occur via presidential signature, becoming law without signature, or veto override. The legislation may be a standalone bill or a provision within other legislation such as the Farm Bill, appropriations, or the NDAA.\n* Court rulings, executive orders, or USDA rulemakings achieving similar effects do not count.\n* A bill that grandfathers existing state laws, applies only prospectively to laws enacted after a certain date, or contains a state opt-out provision will not resolve **Yes**.\n* The effective date of the bill does not matter, as long as it is enacted before July 1, 2027.",
  "background": "The [Save Our Bacon Act (H.R. 4673)](https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/4673) was introduced by Rep. Ashley Hinson (R-IA) on July 23, 2025. It would prohibit states and localities from imposing production-process standards (e.g., minimum confinement space) on livestock products produced outside the state, effectively preempting [California's Proposition 12](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2018_California_Proposition_12) and [Massachusetts' Question 3](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2016_Massachusetts_Question_3). It is similar to the [EATS Act](https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/4797) and traces back to the \"[King Amendment](https://awionline.org/awi-quarterly/2013-fall/king-amendment%E2%80%99s-attack-state-animal-welfare-laws)\" of earlier Congresses.\n\nOn April 30, 2026, the House passed [the Farm, Food, and National Security Act of 2026](https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/7567) (the 2026 Farm Bill) by 224–200, with Save Our Bacon language included. The Rules Committee blocked a bipartisan Luna–Costa amendment that would have stripped the language. The bill is now before the Senate, where Agriculture Committee Chairman John Boozman (R-AR) is shepherding consideration.",
